Your sketches
-------------

The code you write and compile in Gatehouse stays yours (this paragraph is a
summary, not legal advice). The compiled result
contains parts of avr-libc, the Arduino core, the libraries you #include and
libgcc. Their licences apply to those parts: the BSD licence of avr-libc needs
the notice above when you redistribute the binary; the LGPL of the Arduino
core and libraries (Wire, SPI, SoftwareSerial, EEPROM, Servo, NeoPixel) asks
that people who receive your compiled sketch can get the LGPL parts' source
and can relink them (for LGPL v3 libraries, with the other conditions of that
licence). The GCC Runtime Library Exception means libgcc does not make your
program GPL.

How Gatehouse uses these programs
---------------------------------

The tools are separate programs. Gatehouse (MIT licensed) starts each of them
as an independent WebAssembly module in its own memory (a Web Worker in the
browser, Node in tests) and only exchanges files and command-line arguments
with them, the same way a build system calls gcc. Gatehouse does not link
their code into its own and does not modify them. Because of that Gatehouse's
own code stays MIT licensed and the tools stay under their own licences.

Written offer of source code
----------------------------

For the GPL and LGPL parts above (GCC with the Arduino patch, GNU Binutils,
GMP, MPFR, MPC, the Arduino AVR core and the Wire, SPI, SoftwareSerial, EEPROM,
Servo and NeoPixel libraries), Gatehouse offers the complete corresponding
source code, free of charge, at:

  https://github.com/M-KUMG/Gatehouse/releases   (release tag toolchain-sources-v1)

That release contains the source archives (gcc-7.3.0, the Arduino GCC patch,
binutils-2.42, gmp-6.2.1, mpfr-4.1.0, mpc-1.2.1, the Arduino AVR core
